WEBVTT


1
00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:04.420
Have you ever found a knitting
pattern that you love,

2
00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:08.500
but the neckline was too
high or way too low?

3
00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:11.500
Recalculating a round neckline is the next

4
00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:15.260
topic in our Pencil
and Paper Charting series.

5
00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:20.420
If you missed the first three basics
tutorials, you'll want to watch them

6
00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:26.200
first, we will build
on the skills learned.

7
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:32.900
Let's use this front neckline schematic
and make it a little more comfortable.

8
00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:37.860
The pattern gauge is 7stitches
and 10.5 rows per inch.

9
00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.580
The armhole is 9 inches deep

10
00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:45.500
and the neckline in the original
pattern is 4 inches deep.

11
00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:47.600
First thing we want to reset our row

12
00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:52.260
counter to 000 at the beginning
of the armhole shaping.

13
00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:57.660
Now we've decided we want our new
neck depth to be 6 inches deep.

14
00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:04.460
That means we'll knit 3 inches
before we start our shaping.

15
00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:10.620
If we do the math, we'll multiply
three times our row gauge,

16
00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:19.520
do a little rounding and we'll see that
on row 32, we start our neckline shaping.

17
00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:26.460
There will be a total of 64 rows
in the neckline to complete the piece.

18
00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.210
Now the fun starts.

19
00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:33.490
Let's figure out how to create
the curves for our round neck.

20
00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:36.380
Let's start with our graph paper method.

21
00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:38.740
We'll work with half of the neckline.

22
00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:42.460
Mark off 28 stitches by 64 rows.

23
00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:45.380
Remember to use knitting graph paper.

24
00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:49.600
Now sketch a nice curve.

25
00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:53.570
Quickly draw in some stitches
to follow the curve.

26
00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:59.380
Now you'll notice that we're decreasing
three stitches every other row and then

27
00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:05.060
two stitches every other rows,
and then three stitches every fourth row.

28
00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:07.100
This is pretty messy,

29
00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:11.700
difficult to knit, and hard to duplicate
on the other side of the neckline.

30
00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:13.610
There's a better way.

31
00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:17.300
Here's the classic formula
for shaping a neckline.

32
00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:20.100
Bind off half the stitches.

33
00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.340
Decrease the remaining stitches,

34
00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:28.220
one stitch every other row, then knit
straight until the neckline is completed.

35
00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:30.660
This gives you a pretty nice curve.

36
00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:34.500
Remember, this is knitting,
and by the time you attach a band,

37
00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:39.360
you'll never see that this
is a straight line decrease.

38
00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:45.020
Finish by writing your pattern. At row 32,

39
00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:50.260
bind off the center 28 stitches,
working each side separately.

40
00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:52.340
Decrease on the neckline edge,

41
00:02:52.370 --> 00:03:00.340
one stitch every other row 14 times
knit even to row 96.

42
00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:04.020
Even though we did our calculations over
half the neckline,

43
00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:09.020
it's common practice to bind off all
of the center stitches at one time.

44
00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:16.180
This creates a nicer center front and
makes it easier to finish the neckline.

45
00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:21.140
If this method isn't accurate enough
for your taste, there are other formulas

46
00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:24.780
you can use to get a more
gradual and graceful curve.

47
00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:26.300
I recommend the book

48
00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:31.100
Knitwear Design Workshop as
an excellent resource for designing.

49
